putting the ink on the world's largest  free trade agreement  in a virtual signing ceremony  the deal was sealed on the sidelines of  the asean summit  hosted by vietnam  the agreement brings together 15  asia-pacific countries  with a population of more than 2 billion  people  accounting for some 30 percent of the  global economy  the partnership was nearly a decade in  the making  it includes china the world's second  largest economy  but it leaves out top economic power the  united states  president donald trump quit previous  efforts to form a trans-pacific trade  bloc  members of this new pact hope the  further loosening of trade barriers  will lead to greater prosperity for the  asia-pacific region  this agreement signifies that our region  which has been the most  economically dynamic region of the world  in recent decades  is still committed to openness and to  trade  and that we will use that as a platform  and a springboard for recovery in the  post-covert era  the agreement is set to reduce tariffs  harmonize trade rules and streamline  supply chains  analysts say it may cement china's  position as asia's leading economic  power  allowing beijing to call the shots on  important trading terms from japan to  new zealand  china also stands to benefit by being  able to reduce its dependence on other  markets  such as the united states  to talk a little more about the  significance of this pact i'm joined now  by clifford coonan from dw business  clifford thanks for coming in  this is branded as a coup for china how  significant is it really for beijing  i think it's massively significant for  beijing this is  30 of global gdp and in the driving seat  very much as china um china uses trade  as a way to  um to outweigh various um  regional tensions politically and use  its economic muscle to get over the  political strains with a lot of its  neighbors over  uh the south china sea and um and other  areas  um you know the the it doesn't include  things that beijing is less happy about  talking about such environmental goals  or  anything to do with political areas such  as human rights so it's a big win for  beijing  how much does the united states stand to  lose by being excluded from this deal  well i think it's it's very significant  the us isn't in it it's a sign of what's  been going on the last four years with  the u.s looking inwards  um and it's we've seen other trade  trade deals fall because of this and uh  would  move away from multilateralism towards  uh just  looking inwards for the u.s um it's  going to be interesting to see now how  this how this will play out with joe  biden because he's  uh with the president-elect because he's  um presumably  more trade-friendly going on previous  democratic leaders he's more trade  friendly than donald trump  um but how that will play out of course  we'll have to wait and see  but it is significant the u.s isn't in  it because it's a pacific power as well  and and this is supposedly a pacific  deal so it's going to be a big factor  let's look at this new powerful block  from the european perspective  how is it likely to affect the eu well i  think  it will lead to cheaper goods in the in  europe that's one one positive  um it's again putting political pressure  on the eu to see how it's dealing with  china  you know the weighing up the political  versus uh the economic  and so it's going to have to see how it  can get um it can work with this this  new block  because within the block you've got um  the asian con the asean countries these  uh  these southeast asian countries uh with  whom the eu has very strong relations  and then you have  um strategic competitors like china so  there's a there's going to be  a lot of um rebalancing and a lot of  examination about how to move forward  for the eu  one very important economy in asia  that's missing from this trade deal  is india why is that well i think india  is a a big  a big omission or you know the fact it's  not there 